    {
      "key": "primer-basics.html::node-1142",
      "source": "SafeLearning/primer-basics.html",
      "locator": "::node-1142",
      "line": 1236,
      "tag": "p",
      "classes": [],
      "source_text": "Examples. $[0,1]^d$, closed balls and ellipsoids $\\{x:x^\\top Px\\le c\\}$ with $P\\succ0$ (bounded since $x^\\top Px\\ge\\lambda_{\\min}(P)\\|x\\|^2$, Primer A) are compact; $\\mathbb R^n$, $(0,1]$ and $\\{x:x_1^2-x_2^2\\le1\\}$ (closed, unbounded) are not. Sublevel sets need care: $V(x)=x^2/(1+x^2)$ is positive away from $0$, and $\\{V\\le0.5\\}=[-1,1]$, but $\\{V\\le c\\}=\\mathbb R$ for every $c\\ge1$.",
      "text_sha256": "92fa5c248521cfe7524b37c2492cfd9c67dfc986289c8b10ede84ed0ffc538a8",
